/**Using the ‘PROC UNIVARIATE’ to analyze the variable 
‘utilization’ for the following measures: **/

libname hk_data '/home/u63973406/SASCOURSE/hk_data';

data risk;
set hk_data.risk_data;
run;

/**Q1. What are the mean, median, mode and standard deviation? **/

proc univariate data=hk_data.risk_data;
var utilization;
run;
/*---Mean= 31.5911646 Median=15.10000 Mode=0 std=34.9498105---**/

/**Q2. Test if the mean value of ‘utilization’ is 30. **/

proc univariate data=hk_data.risk_data location=30;
var utilization;
run;
/*---see output 'Tests for Location': Mu0=30, p-value is less than 0.01, so we reject Mu0=30---*/

/**Q3. Test if the variable ‘utilization’ is normally distributed. **/

proc univariate data=hk_data.risk_data normal location=30;
var utilization;
run;
/*---see output 'Tests for Normality': p-value is less than 0.01, so 'utilization' is not normally distributed
(reject normality)---*/

/**Q4. Draw the histogram for the variable‘utilization’ with the 
option ‘midpoints=0 to 150 by 10’. What conclusion can you draw 
from the histogram?  */

proc univariate data=hk_data.risk_data normal location=30;
var utilization;
histogram utilization/normal midpoints=0 to 150 by 10;
run;
/*---This histogram displays that the data is not normally distributed, since we see heavy data 
(right skewness/not symmetric) towards the left side of the graph vs. a normally distributed (bell curve) graph. 
This is confirmed by the low p-value (<0.01) in the 'Tests for Noramlity' output, and again confirms that the variable
'utilization' is not normally distributed. --- */ 

